<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baldwin III of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1130–1163</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baldwin III (1130–1163) was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from 1143, at first under the regency of his mother Melisende, whom he pushed aside in 1152. He took part in the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a> in 1148 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 1153, his greatest success. He died childless and was succeeded by his brother Amalric I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baldwin-iii-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Baldwin III of Jerusalem" width="301" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author artist from Paris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baudouin_III_apprenant_la_nouvelle_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
